   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public authorities law, in relation to the publication of the proposed capital program plan by the metropolitan transportation authority",
    "basePrintNo": "S5159",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5159-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1269-b, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5159\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the public authorities law, in relation to the\npublication of the proposed capital program plan by the metropolitan\ntransportation authority\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nCurrent law provides no requirement for the MTA to disclose proposed\ncapital plans to the public, and therefore only the MTA capital\nprogram review board members have access to these plans. This\nlegislation would require the MTA to publish these plans on their\nwebsite as soon as they are presented to board members and committees,\nthereby increasing transparency of the organization.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1: Subdivision 2-a of section 1269 of the Public Authorities\nLaw is amended so that when any proposed capital plan is provided to\nthe committees of the MTA board it is also available at the same time\nto the public on the MTA's website.\n\nSection 2: States this act shall take effect immediately.\n\n EXISTING LAW :\n\nSection 1. Subdivision 2-a of section 1269-b of the Public Authorities\nLaw, provides that any proposed capital program plan offered by the\nMetropolitan Transportation Authority that has been disclosed to the\nindividual committees of the authority for informational purposes must\nalso be disclosed to the capital program review board for\ninformational purposes.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nOn October 28, 2015, the Metropolitan Transportation Authority Board\nvoted on and approved the $29 billion 2015-2019 MTA capital plan, and\ndid so without the public's knowledge of what was being included in\nthe MTA's proposal. The public was therefore unable to participate in\nany open comment period on the proposed capital plan or to otherwise\nscrutinize what was in the budget before the MTA board members voted\non the plan. Details of the approved MTA capital plan were only made\navailable to the public on the MTA's website the next day, only after\nthe MTA board voted to approve the plan.\n\nExisting law does not provide for public disclosure of proposed\ncapital program plans. However, it does require any proposal presented\nto MTA board committees to be made available for New York State's\ncapital program plan review board for informational purposes.  Because\nthe MTA receives taxpayer dollars and works to serve the public's\ntransportation needs, the process by which the capital program plan is\napproved should be transparent and public participation should not be\neffectively barred. This legislation would provide needed transparency\nby requiring the MTA to publically disclose a capital program plan\nproposal on their website at the same the committees of the MTA board\nare presented capital program plan proposals.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n IMPACT ON REGULATION OF BUSINESSES AND INDIVIDUALS :\n\nNone.\n EFFECTS ON FINES, TERMS OF IMPRISONMENT OR OTHER PENAL SANCTIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2016: A10489 -- Referred to Corporations, Authorities and Commissions\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
